  "narrative": "All new values are interpreted exactly as signed to the hypothesis direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency. For direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a higher dependency score, i.e. weaker dependency.\n\nThe specificity tests largely preserve the interpretation of the four validated core associations rather than expanding them broadly. H72 is null for SMARCA4 loss and POLR2A dependency (signed estimate -0.005; 95% interval -0.024 to +0.014), so the large SMARCA4-loss effect appears specific to SMARCA2 rather than spilling into a general transcription-dependency effect. H73 is null for TP53-wild-type status and RPL11 dependency (+0.004; -0.016 to +0.024), supporting the view that the USP7 association is not simply a generic p53/proteostasis or ribosome-stress effect. H74 is null for a main KEAP1-loss effect on USP7 dependency (+0.009; -0.033 to +0.050), reducing concern that the TP53-wild-type USP7 association is confounded by KEAP1-loss co-alteration. H75 is small and imprecise for MSI-high and DHX9 dependency (+0.017; -0.017 to +0.052), supporting specificity of the MSI-high effect to WRN rather than a broad MSI association across dependencies. H78 is null for MTAP loss and USP7 dependency (-0.001; -0.041 to +0.039), and H83 is null for an aneuploidy interaction on the TP53-wild-type USP7 association (-0.014; -0.060 to +0.032), so these genomic-context alternatives are not supported.\n\nThe core-consistency refinements are strong. H76 shows that the MSI-high WRN association remains enormous among BRCA2-intact cells (+0.794; +0.764 to +0.825), complementing the earlier null BRCA2-loss main effect and indicating the WRN association does not require BRCA2-loss co-alteration. H77 shows the TP53-wild-type USP7 association is essentially unchanged among MYC-amplification-negative cells (+0.802; +0.780 to +0.823), indicating the effect is not an artifact of MYC co-alteration. I accept these as dataset-level consistency findings and close them as secondary confirmations of the central associations.\n\nThe new mechanistic screen mostly produced nulls except for PTEN loss. H79 found no meaningful interaction between MSI-high WRN dependency and high mutation burden (-0.026; -0.090 to +0.038), supporting an MSI/MMR-specific rather than generic mutational-burden explanation in this dataset. H80 is null/near-null for NF1 loss and KRAS dependency (-0.020; -0.049 to +0.009), so no RAS-wiring dependency signal emerged there. H83 was null as above. H82 gives a statistically significant negative interaction contrary to the positive hypothesized direction: high hypoxia appears to slightly attenuate the KEAP1-loss reversal of NFE2L2 dependency (-0.054; -0.102 to -0.005). Although the interval excludes zero, the magnitude is small relative to the main reversal near +0.80 and I do not regard this as a scientifically meaningful effect modification worth accepting or validating at this stage; therefore the original positive-direction interaction claim is rejected and closed.\n\nThe important new positive discovery is H81: PTEN loss is associated with stronger STAT3 dependency. The signed estimate is +0.267 with a 95% interval from +0.215 to +0.319, far larger than the typical null/precision bounds in these tests and very large relative to the STAT3 dependency distribution. This is a moderate-to-large dataset-level association and is scientifically meaningful under the 10% clinical-significance context. Given the multiplicity of exploratory comparisons, I do not treat it as validated or causal yet, but I accept it as a current dataset-level association and request independent validation. I also registered follow-up robustness and specificity tests for PTEN loss/STAT3, including batch, Cas9 activity, culture, subtype, PIK3CA interaction, and a KRAS specificity control.",
